ok   we start as root
ok   /bin/mkdir is setuid root
ok   /bin/mv is setuid root
ok   /bin/rmdir is setuid root
ok   /tmp is a directory
ok   guest ran /bin/mkdir to a normal exit
ok   the directory exists, so the effective uid became root
ok   it is owned by guest, so the real uid stayed guest's
ok   and carries guest's group
ok   its mode is 0777
ok   its link count is 2, so it has both . and ..
ok   and the parent gained a link
ok   mknod as guest is refused
ok   unlink of a directory as guest is refused
ok   link to a directory as guest is refused
ok   and guest cannot climb back to root
ok   so nothing was created behind the refusals
ok   guest ran /bin/mv to a normal exit
ok   the renamed directory exists, so the effective uid became root again
ok   it is still owned by guest, so mv never granted the real uid
ok   its link count is still 2
ok   the old name is gone
ok   and a rename within one parent left that parent's link count alone
ok   guest made a subdirectory to move
ok   so its parent gained a link
ok   guest ran /bin/mv across parents to a normal exit
ok   the moved directory exists
ok   it is still owned by guest
ok   and still has both . and ..
ok   the old name is gone
ok   the old parent gave up its link
ok   and the new parent took it
ok   guest ran /bin/rmdir on the moved directory
ok   guest ran /bin/rmdir on the renamed one
ok   both directories are gone
ok   and the parent has its link count back
